\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ Blog:Options for India's New  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Chief\b0\par\par\uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2013\par 11:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Real Time Economics blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/economics }}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Sudeep Jain\par \par \uc2 Markets cheered the appointment of \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2  to head the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  after current governor, Duvvuri Subbarao, retires early next month.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rajan's biggest challenge in the near term is the Indian rupee, which hit another record low of 61.80 to the U.S. dollar on Tuesday as investors take fright over India's mounting current-account deficit and a paralysis in efforts to liberalize the economy.\par \par \uc2 Will he double down on Mr. Subbarao's recent steps to tighten liquidity in the banking system, effectively raising the cost of borrowing, or will he reverse the measures and take other steps to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? India Real Time looks at the options before him.\par \par \uc2 Liquidity measures: If Mr. Rajan decides to continue with Mr. Subbarao's strategy of reducing the supply of cash in the banking system, he has a number of options before him. Currently, the RBI is setting a limit on how much banks can borrow from it daily at 7.25% interest, the main policy rate. If banks need funds beyond the limit, they can either borrow from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a much higher interest rate of 10.25% or borrow from the money markets, where the rates are close to 10.0%. The aim is to make it more expensive to buy U.S. dollars with borrowed rupees. The move has also made government bonds cheaper, which makes them more attractive to foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Frederic Neumann, co-head of Asian economic research at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in Hong Kong, agrees with this policy, given that other reforms to attract foreign investment are unlikely to bear fruit in the near term because of political opposition. "Squeezing short-term rates is the only option they have," he says.\par \par \uc2 To tighten liquidity further, the RBI could increase its cash-reserve ratio or the statutory-liquidity ratio, says Robert Prior-Wandesforde, an economist at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  in Singapore. The cash-reserve ratio is the percentage of deposits that banks need to hold with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the statutory-liquidity ratio is the percentage of deposits that must be invested in low-risk securities, mainly government bonds. Increasing these ratios reduces the amount of money banks have on hand to supply customers at ATMs or for trading. A more aggressive move would be to raise the RBI's key lending rate, says Mr. Prior-Wandesforde. Analysts says the RBI has held off from increasing the policy rate because such a move would indicate that it wants to increase borrowing costs for the entire economy and not just for banks for short durations, a risky move when growth is slowing dramatically.\par \par \uc2 Sovereign Bond Issue: India has issued overseas bonds three times in the past as a way of raising foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilize the rupee. Mr. Subbarao opposed this measure, saying it could compromise India's financial stability. "There is a lot of value to be attached to government's borrowing in domestic markets. We have learnt that lesson during the global financial crisis," he told reporters at a news conference last week. Issuing debt overseas exposes a country to international financial markets. However, Mr. Rajan, who is currently India's chief economic adviser, recently said that an overseas bond issue is one of the options before the government to stabilize the rupee.\par \par \uc2 RBI \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Mr. Subbarao has used \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves sparingly to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts say this is because the RBI has limited room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $280 billion are only sufficient to cover seven months of imports. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also said that it doesn't actively seek to build \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. However, some analysts favor a more muscular approach to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The RBI needs to be more flexible on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and use its reserves when the situation calls for it," says Ashima Goyal, who is a member of an external panel that advises the RBI on monetary policy. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com }}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 07, 2013 05:50 ET (09:50 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130807e987001wm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
