\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ Blog:Emerging Market Central Banks Wheel Out the Big Guns\b0\par\par\uc2 1,290 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2013\par 12:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's MoneyBeat blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Katie Martin and Chiara Albanese\par \par \uc2 Emerging market central banks tried playing nicely. They tried bursts of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now we appear to have reached a new phase in the Great EM \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Selloff, and policymakers are turning to monetary policy to try and turn things around. It's not clear that this will work either. At least, not everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Monday, Turkey signalled that it is set to "bow to the inevitable", in the words of Tim Ash at Standard Bank, lining up a potential interest rate rise next week. The country had dipped repeatedly into its dwindling pot of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to prop up the lira, but it wasn't working out.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday, India joined in, raising borrowing costs under the so-called Marginal Standing Facility by a thumping two percentage points, to 10.25%.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia, nursing an inflation headache but also tackling weakness in the rupiah, also raised rates earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's lira popped a little higher after Monday's announcements. That makes sense--investors have been desperate for a sign that \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might use rates rather than reserves to steady the markets. The rupee also bounced a little Tuesday. But whether monetary policy can really turn this around, particularly in Asia, is a matter of serious doubt.\par \par \uc2 "We are not witnessing a run on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Siddarth Mathur, head of Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and local markets strategy at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in Singapore. The old central banking "textbooks" would have said that tightening monetary policy was the best way to fight this kind of rush of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness, but maybe not now, Mr. Mathur added. "The issue is that the global capital cycle has turned the corner and flows are heading home, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that do not enjoy the support of a large current account surplus will feel the pinch more than others. Central banks are trying to counter this, but it's more about the signal. They can signal that any speculation on top of the real capital flows is unwelcome. This is the message."\par \par \uc2 So, tightening monetary policy makes it more expensive to sell these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it doesn't solve deeper problems.\par \par \uc2 "In India, inflation is already coming off, growth is not exactly red hot. The effect of this could be to scare off equity investors, and that matters a lot for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So this move is not without risks," said Bhanu Baweja, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Baweja, if you recall, is the chap who said back in May that there was "NO WAY" (his caps) it made sense to stand in the way of the emerging markets selloff.\par \par \uc2 The rupee could pick up a little from here for now, but more broadly, "I'm not changing my mind on the back of what the RBI has done. Maybe I'm being a little stubborn," he said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Check out this rundown of other reactions:\par \par \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , Murat Toprak - \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist:"We have to expect more efforts from EM central banks to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In less than two months, their main goal has been reversed. Until May, they were fighting against the appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , now they are trying to counteract the appreciation, hiking interest rates to continue to attract flows.\par \par \uc2 "They are in direct competition with soaring U.S. yields. But will U.S. yields continue to rise? This is not \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's central scenario, and we expect a reversal in the trend.\par \par \uc2 "Policy makers will have to be more responsive and some central banks, including those in India, Turkey and Russia are in a more difficult situation. We are not at panic stage yet, but their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is in a way conventional, is coming in very unconventional markets."\par \par \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 , Christian Lawrence, emerging market strategist: "Central banks have been far more active recently, but not always successful. The problem is that it's much harder to weaken a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than to strengthen it, and this is potentially very costly.\par \par \uc2 "Turkey spent $4.5 billion last week to support the lira, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed the week lower. Turkey used about 20% of its reserves to do that but failed to achieve its goal. \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gone down the same route. This kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue until we see a decent retracement in the markets.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been more clever when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce volatility. It didn't try to stand in the way of the price action, but it waited for it to be on its side.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has increased fears among speculators, who if they are shorting a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk to be caught by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. It is likely that some of them will exit their trades after the Indian announcement and this could reduce volatility in all EM markets."\par \par \uc2 Barclays\uc2 , Igor Arsenin, EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist:"In the case of India, the risk is that portfolio outflows, particularly from equity, will accelerate, neutralizing the measures."\par \par \uc2 Standard Bank, Timothy Ash, emerging market strategist: "Emerging markets are mindful that Fed tapering risks a mad rush for the exit doors, and this mass exodus could be quite disruptive for local markets given the wall of inflows seen over the last three to four years with quantitative easing. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rate hikes are all meant to ease the pace of outflows and make it a more orderly adjustment."\par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 :"Asia policy is turning more hawkish. The RBI has announced measures to tighten liquidity measures (this follows measures taken by \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 ). These measures taken by the RBI are aimed at providing some stability for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an environment of a strengthening dollar. These measures by the RBI to tighten liquidity conditions are also likely to increase downside risks to growth."\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2 , Bhanu Baweja, emerging-markets analyst: "I don't want the rupee to head to 70 (against the dollar) but if the RBI does this, does it make equity investors feel more confident? Or does it make them think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hitting the economy at the wrong time?\par \par \uc2 "This country attracted more equity flows than any other emerging market in the last two or three years. The flows don't even have to reverse in order to hit the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --they just have to slow down.\par \par \uc2 "They are trying to do the right thing, trying to make it expensive to short the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but there can be a cost to pay in terms of growth.\par \par \uc2 "Think about the countries that have done this: Indonesia, Turkey, India, and Brazil. Brazil doesn't count because it started doing this earlier. What links the others is pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. So it's possible that South Africa will take a similar step in a little while."\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , Siddarth Mathur: "Over the last few years, current account surpluses have been whittled away in a number of emerging markets. India has traditionally run a current account deficit, but now it's deeper.\par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't ascribe any evil to the markets. It's not that they are looking for victims. But \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  associated with current account deficits will be at the mercy of global capital flows. This cuts both ways. In times of heavy inflows, central banks stand in the middle to smooth volatility."\par \par \uc2 Write to Katie Martin at katie.martin@dowjones.com; @katie_martin_\b \highlight22\uc2 fx\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 16, 2013 06:47 ET (10:47 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130716e97g001o3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
