\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-UPDATE 2-Indian rupee hits record low, c.bank measures anticipated\b0\par\par\uc2 735 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2013\par 15:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats without any changes to text)\par \par \uc2 * Rupee falls to all-time low of 61.21/dollar\par \par \uc2 * Bond yield hits 2-month highs on foreign outflows\par \par \uc2 * Speculation c.bank may open USD window for oil companies\par \par \uc2 By Subhadip Sircar and Swati Bhat\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, July 8 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee fell to a record low while bond yields surged on Monday, exacerbating fears about the funding of the current account deficit and sending policy makers scrambling to find quick fix solutions beyond sporadic \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  was due to meet with officials from oil companies, the biggest buyers of dollars in domestic markets, to discuss their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs, two sources with direct knowledge of the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the RBI, which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the session, could mandate that refiners buy dollars via a separate window and not in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, a measure that would help ease pressure on the rupee.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Prime Minister \uc2 Manmohan Singh\uc2  will meet industry leaders on July 29 to discuss the rupee, while Finance Minister Palaniappan Chidambaram was due to travel to the United States from Monday in a previously scheduled trip to drum up foreign direct investment, especially in infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Efforts to contain the rupee's slide highlight the vulnerability of a country dependent on capital inflows to fund a current account deficit that hit a record high of 4.8 percent in the fiscal year ended in March.\par \par \uc2 "Weakening rupee, along with rising oil, is not good news for India," said \uc2 Shubhada Rao\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 Yes Bank\uc2  in Mumbai.\par \par \uc2 "As such, rupee will come under continued pressure. We expect policymakers to take more administrative steps to bring some stability to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market."\par \par \uc2 The rupee closed at 60.61/62 after earlier hitting a record low of 61.21, breaching a previous all-time low of 60.76 on June 26. It had closed at 60.225/235 on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The falls were sparked as U.S. jobs data on Friday cemented bets the Federal Reserve will wind down its quantitative easing, while a a spike in oil prices that sent Brent crude futures to a more than three-month high further aggravated concerns about India's current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee has weakened 9.3 percent this year, the worst fall in emerging Asia. The weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sparking foreign sales of bonds and stocks, which in turn is further pressuring the rupee.\par \par \uc2 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ Measures India can take to prevent a rupee slide India cbank cracks down on speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading Cbank to meet oil cos on Monday to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  PM to discuss INR fall with industry on July 29 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\par \par \uc2 BONDS SLUMP\par \par \uc2 Bonds fell to a two-month low with foreign investors having sold more than $7 billion in debt since May 22.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year yield ended up 7 basis points at 7.57 percent from its previous close, after earlier rising to as much as 7.63 percent, its highest since May 9.\par \par \uc2 The expected market volatility led the \uc2 Fixed Income Money Market and Derivatives Association of India\uc2  to remove trading bands for the day, a measure it also adopted when the rupee was last plumbing record lows late last month.\par \par \uc2 The 5-year overnight indexed year surged as much as 15 bps to 7.69 percent, a two-year high, before closing up 6 bps on the day at 7.60 percent.\par \par \uc2 The 1-year rate ended up 5 bps at 7.55 percent, further widening the gap between the two after the spread between the two rates turned positive on Friday for the first time in two years, normalising the disinverted rate curve.\par \par \uc2 The RBI was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in both the morning and afternoon sessions to defend the rupee. Yet traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need stronger action to have a longer-lasting impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Among measures beyond those targeting oil companies, the RBI could curb speculative positions by imposing limits on intraday or overnight net open position limits, they said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the government could review limits for foreign investment in sectors such as defence or issue a sovereign bond to non-resident Indians, dealers said. (Editing by Rafael Nam, Richard Borsuk and Ron Popeski)\par \par \uc2 INDIA-MARKETS/ (UPDATE 2) (REPEAT) | ABX | SNS | SNI | ABN | D | E | RBN | M   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130711e978000qv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
