\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Rupee leads Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  losses as solid U.S. jobs spur fears of outflows\b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2013\par 04:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices. For midday report, double-click ) SINGAPORE, July 8 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee hit a record low on Monday, leading declines among emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as better-than-expected U.S. jobs growth boosted expectations that the Federal Reserve will reduce its stimulus measures, driving up U.S. treasuries' yields and heightening worries about capital outflows from the region. Sentiment about emerging Asia was also hit by China's plan to choke off credit to force consolidation in industries plagued by overcapacity as it seeks to end the economy's reliance on investment funded by cheap debt. The rupee fell to an all-time low of 61.21 per dollar, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  is vulnerable to the sea-change in capital flows as it needs inflows to fund its big current account deficit. The Singapore dollar hit a 13-month low against the greenback, while the Thai baht touched an 11-month trough.\par \par \uc2  Asian financial markets suffered after U.S. non-farm payroll data that showed 195,000 jobs were created last month, better than the 165,000 expected. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0710 GMT\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen       100.96         101.19       +0.23\par   Sing dlr        1.2832         1.2811       -0.16\par   Taiwan dlr    