\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ Blog:Five Ways to Arrest the Rupee's Decline\b0\par\par\uc2 942 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2013\par 06:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's India Real Time Report blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/indiarealtime/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/indiarealtime/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Sudeep Jain\par \par \uc2 With the rupee close to 60 per U.S. dollar, Indian authorities have been trying to assuage investors, but how effective are their options?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  has "a lot of firepower," according to Montek Singh Ahluwalia, deputy chairman of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Planning Commission, and it has already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at least twice, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say.\par \par \uc2 Even so, the rupee was trading at 59.71 to the dollar Tuesday morning, just shy of Thursday's record low of 59.97.\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at the RBI's arsenal:\par \par \uc2 Sell more dollars: The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could potentially sell more dollars to keep the rupee high. But its ability to do so is limited by the size of India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which stand at $290 billion. This covers only seven months worth of imports, which is already too low or comfort, said Indranil Sen Gupta, an economist at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  in Mumbai. Reserves as a proportion of imports haven't been so low since in 1996.\par \par \uc2 Issue dollar bonds: India has a huge base of non-resident Indians whose remittances contribute to narrowing the current account gap, which is roughly the difference between imports and exports. In the 1990s, when India was facing pressure on its balance of payments, the government sold dollar bonds to non-residents and used the money to fund its current account gap.\par \par \uc2 The "India Development Bonds," "Resurgent India Bonds" and "India Millennium Deposits" helped raise around $11 billion between 1991 and 2000. The bonds were issued in a foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the U.S. dollar and the British pound, and repaid in that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so investors didn't have to take \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. Analysts say if similar bonds were issued now they'd be popular because individuals would earn higher interest rates than in the U.S. or U.K.\par \par \uc2 India hasn't indicated that it will issue such bonds, but the possibility has increased because of the rupee's weakness \uc2 Barclays\uc2  said in a recent research note.\par \par \uc2 Raise foreign investment in bonds: This has been the favorite tool of Indian authorities to attract more foreign capital over the past few quarters. Foreign investors can buy only a limited quantity of \uc2 Indian government\uc2  bonds, but the quota has been gradually raised over the last 12 months, from $15 billion to $30 billion. The most recent increase in the limit came two weeks ago when the government allowed entities like sovereign wealth funds and pension and insurance funds to invest an additional $5 billion in \uc2 Indian government\uc2  bonds, taking the limit to $30 billion.\par \par \uc2 Authorities could potentially lift the limit again, but economists don't think that will help, given that foreign investors have already pulled $4.2 billion out of Indian debt in June alone as they rush to higher yields on U.S. bonds and worry that India's economic problems could hurt potential returns on investments here.\par \par \uc2 "The focus has to be on attracting long-term rather than short-term capital," said \uc2 Sonal Varma\uc2 , India economist at brokerage firm Nomura.\par \par \uc2 Attract more foreign direct investment: Government officials have said they are working on opening up Indian industry to greater foreign investment, bringing more foreign capital into India.\par \par \uc2 "The weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has increased the probability of another round of reforms," Nomura said in a recent research note. It said defense, telecom, commodity exchanges and asset reconstruction companies were sectors that could see an increase in foreign investment limits. But allowing foreigners to invest more doesn't necessarily mean money will come rushing in. Last year, India lifted foreign investment limits in retail, aviation and broadcasting companies as part of a broader effort to boost growth. But the sectors haven't really seen much investment since, partly because companies are put off by the red tape and various conditions imposed by the government.\par \par \uc2 Allowing greater foreign investment in Indian companies is a positive step, but it isn't likely to help the rupee right away.\par \par \uc2 Raise interest rates: Some analysts are calling for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise India's interest rates to make Indian debt more attractive to foreign investors. "Now India needs to have a strong monetary policy if it wants to stem the fall" of the rupee, said Suresh Kumar Ranganathan, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Malaysia-based bank \uc2 CIMB Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some academic research makes a case for raising interest rates when a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciates, also because it would reduce inflationary expectations. A weakening rupee makes Indian imports more costly, and if unchecked could lead to higher prices within the country.\par \par \uc2 During the Asian crises of 1997-98, the RBI raised its benchmark interest rate by three percentage points in one go to 8%, in a bid to attract capital from foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 This time around, the RBI won't be in a hurry to take such a measure because a rate increase could worsen India's economic slowdown. The economy grew only 5.0% last fiscal year through March, its slowest pace in a decade.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left benchmark interest rates unchanged, saying that only a sustained decline in inflation would cause it to lower rates.\par \par \uc2 -- Kenan Machado and Rajesh Roy contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Follow India Real Time on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @IndiaRealTime.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 25-06-13 0444GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130625e96p0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
