\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Rupee Nears 60 a Dollar Despite RBI  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Khushita Vasant and Ashutosh Joshi  \par \uc2 806 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 2013\par 14:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI--The Indian rupee fell to a new low against the U.S. dollar on Thursday, as global investors pulled out of emerging markets broadly amid concerns over the scaling back of a U.S. program that has injected billions of dollars into the global financial system.\par \par \uc2 The rupee fell to a low of 59.97 for one U.S. dollar late Thursday, despite an attempt by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  to stem the fall in the morning, according to several \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers in Mumbai.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  early Thursday when the rupee was trading at 59.85 for one U.S. dollar, according to three dealers. That had helped the rupee gain some value, but it weakened further later in the day. The RBI likely stepped in again in the afternoon when the rupee fell below 59.90 rupees for one dollar, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers told The Wall Street Journal. It was trading at 59.58 for one dollar by late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has now fallen more than 10% since the start of May, making it one of the worst-performing major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, investors were not calmed by statements made by Indian officials who said that the finance ministry and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were ready to take steps to help stabilize the rupee, but didn't specify any measures.\par \par \uc2 "India needs to do more in terms of action to signal it will limit the fall of the rupee," said Suresh Kumar Ramanathan, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Kuala Lumpur-based financial services firm \uc2 CIMB Group\uc2 . "The weakening of the rupee is quite dangerous" for India, Mr. Ramanathan added.\par \par \uc2 A falling rupee makes imports more expensive, and can add to inflation within India. Foreign institutional investors have pulled around $3.75 billion from Indian stocks and bonds in June, according the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Board of India\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The rupee's sharp decline also sent Indian stocks and bonds tumbling on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Indian shares posted their biggest single-day decline in nearly 16 months, with the 30-share \uc2 Bombay Stock Exchange\uc2  Sensex dropping 526.41 points, or 2.7%, to 18719.29 points. The National Stock Exchange's Nifty index fell 166.35 points, or 2.9%, to 5655.90 points.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, yields on the benchmark 10-year government bond spiked as bond investors rushed to sell their holdings as they feared that bond prices could fall further. When bond prices fall, bond yields rise.\par \par \uc2 Bond trading came to a halt for several minutes on Thursday morning, as there were no buyers for bonds, according to dealers. The benchmark 8.15% bond that is due in 2022 saw its yield jump to 7.64% by Thursday afternoon, versus 7.47% at Wednesday's close-a big move in the bond world.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's selloff in Indian markets was triggered by an announcement by the U.S. Federal Reserve which said that the U.S. economy was improving, and indicated that this could lead to a reversal of its existing program of buying bonds worth $85 billion a month.\par \par \uc2 This program had led to a flush of capital in the global financial system in recent years. Investors fear that as this program is rolled back, global funds will reallocate their investments to the U.S. dollar and equities, which now have better prospects and are considered a safer bet.\par \par \uc2 Such withdrawal has already led to sales of stocks and bonds across emerging markets and Asia.\par \par \uc2 "It is a panic reaction," said Neelesh Surana, head of equities with Mirae Asset Global Investments (India) Pvt., a money-management firm in Mumbai.\par \par \uc2 For India, the selloff has been sharper because of its inherent economic problems, including a wide current-account deficit and slowing growth. Also, investors have been frustrated at the pace of economic reforms, saying the government hasn't opened up Indian industry enough for fresh foreign direct investments.\par \par \uc2 Investors are now worried that the rupee's sharp slide would exacerbate problems for India. "A falling rupee could further [widen] our current-account deficit," said Alex Mathews, head of research at brokerage \uc2 Geojit BNP Paribas Financial Services\uc2  in Kochi, Kerala.\par \par \uc2 Investors fear that the rupee's decline may not be yet over.\par \par \uc2 "Given the pace of the slide in the rupee since early May, a move to 62 or 63 to a dollar cannot be ruled out," said Mitul Kotecha, a Hong Kong-based global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at investment bank \uc2 Credit Agricole\uc2  CIB.\par \par \uc2 -Kenan Machado, Sudeep Jain and Gurdev Singh Virk contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Khushita Vasant Khushita.Vasant@dowjones.com and Ashutosh Joshi at Ashutosh.Joshi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 20, 2013 08:09 ET (12:09 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130620e96k002ho\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
