\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Rupiah weak despite c.bank deposit rate rise, outflows hit baht\b0\par\par\uc2 1,165 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2013\par 03:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Indonesia c.bank makes surprise 25 bps hike in FASBI rate     * \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 : ready to supply dollars to support rupiah     * Thai finmin says not worried about fund outflows   (Adds text, updates prices)     By Jongwoo Cheon     SINGAPORE, June 12 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah stayed weak on Wednesday despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise increase in its overnight facility rate and its pledge to provide more dollar liquidity, while the Thai baht hit a nine-month low on capital outflows.     \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  announced late on Tuesday that it was raising the FASBI rate, which it pays commercial banks for deposits, by 25 basis points to 4.25 percent, effective Wednesday.       The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday said it was ready to ensure large supplies of dollars to support a weakening rupiah and to buy government bonds in the secondary market.        But the rupiah's spot indicative price fell 0.3 percent to 9,850 per dollar.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded weaker than that level with some dollar bids seen above 10,000 earlier, according to traders.     "BI apparently felt that they couldn't wait until tomorrow. They wanted to react before things moved too quickly," said Sean Yokota, head of Asia strategy at Scandinavian bank SEB in Singapore, referring to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's scheduled policy meeting on Thursday.     On Tuesday, the rupiah fell below 10,000 to the dollar for the first time since 2009, as dollar liquidity tightened up on speculation that a tapering of monetary stimulus by the Federal Reserve may cause capital flight from emerging Asia.     The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also spotted providing dollars for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah.     Indonesia and \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  are the most vulnerable among emerging Asian markets to capital outflows, triggered by expectations of higher U.S. interest rates, as these countries run massive current account deficits that are financed primarily by foreign portfolio investments.     Notwithstanding a sell-off in the past three weeks, foreigners have not been liquidating Indonesian government bonds too heavily, and they still hold around 34 percent of them, near the record level.     Analysts suspect a large part of the pressure on the rupiah is being felt in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as investors hold onto their high-yielding assets but seek some protection against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility.     Some analysts said \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's efforts may relieve the rupiah.     Reflecting such views, the rupiah forwards recovered earlier losses with one-month dollar/rupiah non-deliverable forwards  a tad lower in the afternoon after rising.     \uc2 HSBC\uc2  said the rupiah may find more support if the government finalises a long-awaited reduction in fuel subsidies.     The bank said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to raise the FASBI rate "was unexpected, but a step in the right direction."     "In the next few days, if there is finally an announcement on fuel subsidy cuts, this could give market sentiment towards IDR assets another shot in the arm," it wrote in a client note.     Indonesia has been agonising for months over how to cut fuel price subsidies that are devouring the budget and widening the country's current account deficit. But the reduction is seen accelerating inflation at the same time. An increase in fuel prices is generally expected, but the timing has been uncertain.     Still, the rupiah is not free from speculation about the Federal Reserve tapering monetary stimulus. The expectations have supported the U.S. dollar.     Gundy Cahyadi of \uc2 OCBC Bank\uc2  said that how the rupiah fares will depend on how the dollar broadly performs. "If U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar spikes to 1.28 or something, one will expect to see dollar/rupiah much higher despite the FASBI hike."     The city-state's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  barely changed at 1.2552 to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     \par     BAHT\par     The baht lost 0.7 percent to 31.17 per dollar, its weakest\par since Sept. 11, 2012, pressured by outflows from Thai stocks and\par bonds.\par     Finance Minister Kittirat Na Ranong said he was not worried\par about recent capital outflows because they could help balance\par flows and the Thai baht. \par     Bangkok stocks hit a near five-month low, while bonds lost\par ground.\par     The 10-year government bond yield jumped to 4.11\par percent, its highest since March 2010. The five-year yields rose\par to 3.37 percent, the highest since October last year.\par     "The equities and bonds sell-off still continues," said a\par senior Thai bank trader in Bangkok, adding that the baht is\par weakening to the previous low of 31.25 in the short term.\par     \par     WON\par     The won edged higher on demand from exporters such as\par shipbuilders for settlements.\par     Earlier, the South Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose as much as 0.6\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 percent with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Heavy Industries Co Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par announcing it had won a 1.46 trillion won ($1.29 billion) order.\par  \par     The finance ministry also played down market concerns about\par a potential surge in capital outflows, saying that foreign\par central banks and institutional investors continued to buy local\par debt and showed no signs of pulling out. \par     Still, the won gave up most of its initial gains as foreign\par investors kept selling Seoul shares. Offshore funds also\par reduced won holdings, traders said.\par     Foreigners were net sellers for a fourth consecutive session\par in Seoul's main stock market. They dumped a combined net\par 2.2 trillion won during those four days, according to Korea\par Exchange Data.\par     "Dollar demand from custodian banks and offshore funds stood\par out. Investors tried to buy dollar/won whenever it fell," said a\par South Korean bank trader in Seoul.\par         \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par   Change on the day at 0720 GMT\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par   Japan yen        96.74          96.04       -0.72\par   Sing dlr        1.2539         1.2549       +0.08\par  *Taiwan dlr      30.121         30.160       +0.13\par   Korean won     1133.30        1134.00       +0.06\par   Baht             31.12          30.96       -0.51\par  *Peso             43.20          43.20       +0.00\par   Rupiah         9850.00        9820.00       -0.30\par   Rupee            58.02          58.39       +0.64\par   Ringgit         3.1355         3.1500       +0.46\par  *Yuan            6.1335         6.1335       -0.00\par  \par   Change so far in 2013\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par   Japan yen        96.74          86.79      -10.29\par   Sing dlr        1.2539         1.2219       -2.55\par   Taiwan dlr      30.121         29.136       -3.27\par   Korean won     1133.30        1070.60       -5.53\par   Baht             31.12          30.61       -1.64\par   Peso             43.20          41.05       -4.98\par   Rupiah         9850.00        9630.00       -2.23\par   Rupee      