\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Seen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\uc2  to Help Rupee\b0\par\par\uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2013\par 14:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI--\b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely sold U.S. dollars in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Tuesday, creating a temporary respite for the battered rupee \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it plumbed to new lows for the second straight day.\par \par \uc2 Deep concerns over India's economic direction, however, mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to remain under pressure, traders said.\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  appeared to sell dollars to stop the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breaching 59 rupees. The bank doesn't comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but large orders put through state banks--as seen Tuesday--typically point toward \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The action brought some relief to the rupee, which is one of the worst-performing emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year due to concerns about India's slowing economy and large current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 After trading just short of 59 per rupee, the U.S. dollar retreated to 58.40 on Tuesday afternoon following the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was still trading higher than 58.15 late Monday and off 8% against the dollar since early May.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action may slow the rupee's decline but it is unlikely to change the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fortunes.\par \par \uc2 "They've done well to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Marc Ostwald, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Monument Securities Ltd\uc2 ., a brokerage in London. "Does it turn around the rupee? Definitely not."\par \par \uc2 Investors are worried about India's large current account deficit, which was equal to 6.7% of gross domestic product in the final quarter of 2012. India's exports have stagnated amid weak global demand, while imports of gold and oil have remained high, widening the deficit.\par \par \uc2 The government relies on foreign investment flows to bridge the gap. But foreign investors have pulled out $2.5 billion since late May. That compares with inflows of $4.49 billion for the first five months of 2013.\par \par \uc2 In part, the outflows reflect a selloff across emerging markets as investors move more money into dollar-denominated assets. But India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has underperformed others in Asia, signaling investor frustration with the country's failure to follow through on promises to allow more foreign investment in its ailing economy.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, \uc2 Raghuram Rajan\uc2 , the government's chief economic adviser, said he believed the slump in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had outweighed the economy's fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Rajan said recent efforts the government has taken to reduce demand for gold imports, which include raising import taxes on gold, are starting to bear fruit.\par \par \uc2 "With the steps which the government has taken we will see a significant drop in gold imports in June," he said.\par \par \uc2 These steps fall short of economic overhauls that could unleash a wave of foreign investment in sectors ranging from retail to insurance, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Radhika Rao, an economist at DBS Group in Singapore, said in a note that while broad dollar strength has triggered the rupee's fall to record lows, "the domestic negative macroeconomic backdrop serves to hasten the bear-run." The weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is dampening expectations the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut rates when it meets June 17. Businesses have been calling for a sharp cut to give a boost to economic growth, which has slowed to 5%, much below annual rates of 9% a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 Inflation also has moderated recently, raising hopes of a rate cut. The next set of inflation data, due Friday, is expected to show that wholesale prices, the benchmark in India, remained below 5% in May.\par \par \uc2 That would have made a stronger case for rate cuts. But the rupee's slump has raised the specter of higher inflation down the road as imports become more costly.\par \par \uc2 "We were expecting a rate cut in May," said Deepak Panjwani, head of fixed income at GEPL Securities, a Mumbai-based brokerage. "But given what's happening with the rupee, a rate cut looks difficult."\par \par \uc2 -Shefali Anand contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Khushita Vasant at khushita.vasant@dowjones.com [ 06-11-13 0825ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020130611e96b0002m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
