\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Stocks, Dollar Slump Amid Fed Worries\b0\par\par\uc2 By Matthew Walter and Neelabh Chaturvedi   \par \uc2 948 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2013\par 21:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks fell and the dollar slumped, extending a bout of market turmoil as investors puzzled out the implications of a possible tightening of \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  easy-money policies.\par \par \uc2 The sharp moves appeared to be a resumption of the volatility seen overnight as investors fretted that the U.S. Federal Reserve would soon move to cut back on the money it's been pumping into the economy.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average was down 79 points to 15160.30, while the dollar traded down 2.7% at 96.12 yen, on track for one of its biggest percentage declines in the last three years. Treasurys, which had slumped earlier Tuesday, pushed higher as investors shed riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 The afternoon selling marked the second reversal for financial markets on Tuesday. Earlier, stocks, bonds and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Philippines to Spain to the U.S. came under pressure. A selloff in government-bond markets across Europe led to higher yields from Greece to Germany.\par \par \uc2 Financial markets had stabilized during New York trading hours, but turned south again in the afternoon, underscoring how a lack of clarity about the Fed's thinking has made markets more volatile.\par \par \uc2 "People are less certain today than they have been in the last two years," said Peter Gorra, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in New York. "They're looking over the edge and they're not sure what's on the other side."\par \par \uc2 Traders pinned the blame for the market's ups and downs on sensitivity to signals from central bankers, in particular the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and the Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., investors have been rattled by Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke's suggestion last month that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering a move to wind down its bond purchases.\par \par \uc2 While officials have tried to send signals in recent weeks that any changes will be gradual, traders are increasingly convinced the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next move will be to begin tapering the pace of its bond buying program, removing a key source of support for asset prices even as concerns about a slowdown in global growth are mounting.\par \par \uc2 "The recent uptick in volatility across financial markets looks increasingly as if it might be to do with a growing loss of confidence in Western central bankers' great monetary policy experiment," said Jeremy Batstone-Carr at Charles Stanley.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year Treasury note, which rises as bond prices fall, rose as high as 2.291% earlier in the session-the highest level in more than a year, according to Tradeweb. Treasurys later rebounded, pulling the yield back to 2.195%.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, in Japan, investors looking to see whether the BOJ would ramp up its asset-buying program were disappointed after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from introducing new measures<{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com/article/SB10001424127887324634304578538661580938482.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com/article/SB10001424127887324634304578538661580938482.html}}}\uc2 >, citing signs of economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Stocks saw their steepest declines in Asia, with shares off 4.6% in the Philippines-the biggest drop since September 2011-and 3.1% in Indonesia. European and Japanese stocks also traded lower. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average fell 1.5%, after rallying 4.9% in the previous session.\par \par \uc2 Emerging-markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Brazil's real to South Africa's rand also gained ground after being hammered earlier in the day along with global stock markets. Both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had hit four-year lows earlier.\par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee traded at a record low as the dollar strengthened to nearly 59 rupees before the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while central banks in Brazil and Turkey also stepped into the market to stabilize their exchange rates. The Australian dollar, another high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has fallen sharply in recent weeks, dropped as low at US$0.9325, its weakest level since September 2010.\par \par \uc2 The yield on Greece's 10-year benchmark government bond rose 0.90 percentage point to 10.35% Tuesday, hitting its highest level since early May, according to Tradeweb. The yield on the corresponding Portuguese government bond surged 0.34 percentage point to 6.52%, a two-month peak. Italian and Spanish bond yields also rose. Haven German Bund yields climbed, albeit more modestly than their lower-rated euro-zone peers.\par \par \uc2 Market watchers said they expect the volatility to continue until the Fed lays out how and when it will end its stimulus. The Fed next meets on June 18 and 19.\par \par \uc2 "Whether it's six months down the line or 12 months down the line, it's going to be tapering," said Geoffrey Yu, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "Once that happens, we're going to find a base in different asset classes."\par \par \uc2 -Daniel Inman and Albert Yoon contributed to this article\par \par \uc2 Write to Matthew Walter at matthew.walter@dowjones.com and Neelabh Chaturvedi at neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This item was corrected at 3:34 p.m. EDT to show that the dollar traded down 2.7% at 96.12 yen, on track for one of its biggest percentage declines in the last three years. The original incorrectly stated the yen traded 2.7% down.\par \par \uc2 11 Jun 2013 15:33 EDT DJ  Correction to Stocks, Dollar Slump Amid Fed Worries Story\par \par \uc2 "Stocks, Dollar Slump Amid Fed Worries," published at 3:15 p.m. EDT, incorrectly stated in the third paragraph that the yen traded down 2.7%. The dollar traded down 2.7% at 96.12 yen, on track for one of its biggest percentage declines in the last three years.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 11, 2013 15:33 ET (19:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130611e96b004hm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
