\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Indian Rupee Hits Fresh Low Against the Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 By Khushita Vasant   \par \uc2 897 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2013\par 08:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI--India's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a fresh record low against the dollar Tuesday on rising concerns over a widening trade imbalance and the pace of promised economic steps.\par \par \uc2 The rupee was trading at 58.85 rupees to the U.S. dollar compared with 58.15 rupees in late Asian trade Monday, its previous lowest level.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  broadly have been declining as investors move money into U.S. assets. The dollar is in favor following a string of better-than-expected U.S. economic data over the past few weeks. On Monday, \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  revised its U.S. credit outlook to stable from negative.\par \par \uc2 But the rupee is among the most battered regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to concerns over its current-account gap. Oil and gold imports contributed to a deficit equal to 6.7% of gross domestic product in the final quarter of 2012.\par \par \uc2 Radhika Rao, an economist at DBS Group in Singapore, said in a note that while broad dollar strength has triggered the rupee's fall to record lows, "the domestic negative macroeconomic backdrop serves to hasten the bear-run."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rao said she doesn't expect the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in a large way but it may sell dollars in an attempt to reduce volatility.\par \par \uc2 While a rise in the dollar has hurt the rupee, emerging-market investors attributed the broader weakness in the Indian rupee to the country's ballooning current-account deficit. They say the deficit has become emblematic of the troubles in Asia's second-largest economy, which is suffering from slowing growth and high inflation. India's exports remain sluggish because global demand is weak, while a run-up in gold purchases is keeping imports elevated.\par \par \uc2 India for months has relied on inflows from foreign investment funds to bridge this gap and stabilize its markets.\par \par \uc2 That support is ebbing, however, as some money managers lose patience with a government that they say has been slow to implement new rules aimed at attracting foreign investment and spurring growth.\par \par \uc2 "India's fundamentals aren't robust," says David Robbins, a managing director with \uc2 TCW Group Inc\uc2 . who oversees the fund manager's $7.4 billion emerging-markets portfolio. "Clearly, the government has to do more."\par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have pulled $1.59 billion from India's bond market this month, according to the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Board of India\uc2 . That compares with inflows of $4.49 billion for the first five months of 2013.\par \par \uc2 The downward pressure on Indian assets has been exacerbated by the recent selloff across emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 But the rupee has underperformed other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which some investors say underscores the greater challenges facing India. The rupee has weakened 7.5% against the dollar since the start of May. Over the same period, the Thai baht has dropped 5.1% and the Philippine peso is down 4%.\par \par \uc2 The experience of \uc2 IKEA\uc2  shows India's bumpy road to reform. In 2012, India amended its laws to allow foreign retailers to have 100% ownership of their stores. But government agencies delayed the approval of the Swedish furniture retailer's $1.95 billion investment plan, which was approved in May, nearly 10 months after it was submitted.\par \par \uc2 Indian officials also pledged to raise fuel prices, which are heavily regulated, to reduce government spending on subsidies and to encourage drivers to cut consumption. But fuel prices remain well below prices on the world market, and the cost of oil imports remains high. This continues to cause a drag on the current account, which hit a record deficit of $32.6 billion in the three months ended Dec. 31, 2012.\par \par \uc2 "Given the situation, we expect the rupee to remain under pressure, at least for the next three months," said Mahendra Kumar Jajoo, chief investment officer at Pramerica Asset Managers Pvt. Ltd., which oversees about $447 million of assets.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, some investors say India's economy will gain from the government's efforts, even if progress has been uneven so far.\par \par \uc2 "India has been taking the right steps in the right direction," says Teresa Kong, portfolio manager of the $68 million Matthews Asia Strategic Income Fund. She has been buying the rupee recently, and is looking to add exposure after Monday's sharp fall. "It's not clear to me that India is worse off today than it was six months ago or a year ago." She adds that the fall in world oil prices since the start of the year could translate into a positive surprise, in terms of a narrowing of the current-account deficit, later this year.\par \par \uc2 As well, some Indians based in the Middle East are capitalizing on the rupee's fall by bringing money back to India, said N.S. Venkatesh, the head of treasury at state-run \uc2 IDBI Bank Ltd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The selling of dollars could lift the rupee in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Still, Indian importers, including oil companies, are buying dollars amid worries that more weakness in the rupee will boost their costs. "The panic has set in," said Kishore Narne, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading in Mumbai at Motilal Oswal Securities.\par \par \uc2 Write to 