\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ India  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Governor: Retail Inflation High, Economy Still a Concern\b0\par\par\uc2 740 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Mai 2013\par 12:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 30 May 2013 06:55 EDT *DJ  India RBI Governor: Upside Risk for Inflation Exists\par \par \uc2 30 May 2013 07:19 EDT *DJ  RBI Governor: Recent Rupee Depreciation Due to Large Current-Account Deficit\par \par \uc2 30 May 2013 07:29 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  RBI Governor: Will \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Manage \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Volatility\par \par \uc2 30 May 2013 07:32 EDT *DJ  India RBI: Will Consider Current-Account Deficit While Formulating Monetary Policy\par \par \uc2 30 May 2013 07:32 EDT *DJ  India RBI Governor: Attracting Stable, Non-Debt Capital a Challenge\par \par \uc2 30 May 2013 07:46 EDT *DJ  India RBI Governor: High Level of Bad Loans Disturbing, but Situation Will Improve\par \par \uc2 30 May 2013 10:43 EDT DJ  India RBI Warns on Retail Inflation, Current-Account Deficit\par \par \uc2 By Romit Guha\par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI--India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is concerned about the country's wide current-account deficit and still-high retail inflation, Governor Duvvuri Subbarao said Thursday, damping hopes that it would continue to lower interest rates to support economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 10-month low and bond prices fell to its lowest in two weeks as the markets were expecting the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  would cut its policy lending rate at its June 17 meeting to help boost economic growth that has slowed to its weakest pace in a decade.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Subbarao, while speaking at an event in the western Indian city of Ahmedabad, said the RBI will factor in the current-account gap while formulating its monetary policy. He said the wide deficit is weakening the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and may fan inflation that has slowed in recent months.\par \par \uc2 The bank is concerned about the size of the deficit as well as the way India funds it, he said.\par \par \uc2 India's current-account deficit is estimated to be around 5% of gross domestic product in the last fiscal year ended March 31, compared with the previous year's 4.2%. The gap widened to a record 6.7% of GDP in the October-December quarter, the latest period for which the number is available.\par \par \uc2 The big deficit underscores the impact of the weak global markets on India's exports of goods and services. India's imports, especially of oil and gold, remain high. The country funds much of the deficit with foreign investments in its stock and bond markets, which authorities and analysts say could become tricky as these investments are usually short term.\par \par \uc2 "The result of the high current-account deficit has been the depreciation of the rupee," he said. "Rupee depreciation is a problem because it pushes up the cost of our imports, it increases debt servicing, it causes inflation and it erodes our external payment situation," he added.\par \par \uc2 Thursday, the Indian rupee fell to 56.38 to a dollar, a level not seen since July 25. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hit by concerns that the U.S. may reduce its bond-purchase program, which has emerged as a key source of funds for investments in emerging markets such as India.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Subbarao said India's retail inflation is still a concern. Sacrificing growth in the short term is "inevitable" as the RBI strives to rein in inflation, he added.\par \par \uc2 Consumer inflation slowed to 9.39% in April from 10.39% a month earlier, but is still high. However, the wholesale-price inflation, the main gauge of inflation in India, eased in April to below 5%, a level the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it is comfortable with.\par \par \uc2 The RBI cut its key lending rate thrice this year, all by a quarter of a percentage point, and markets were hoping it to further reduce the rate at its next policy meeting.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark 8.15% bond due in 2022 rose to 7.45% in late trade, a two-week high, before closing at 7.44%, compared with 7.39% on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Write to Romit Guha at romit.guha@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This item was corrected at 11:56 a.m. EDT. The original misstated current-account deficit as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -account deficit in the first paragraph.\par \par \uc2 30 May 2013 11:55 EDT DJ  Correction to India RBI Story\par \par \uc2 "India RBI Warns on Retail Inflation, Current-Account Deficit," at 1443 GMT, misstated current-account deficit as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -account deficit in the first paragraph.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 30, 2013 11:55 ET (15:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130530e95u00212\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
