\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-India rupee hits record low; on watch for RBI\b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2012\par 07:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI, June 22 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee hit a record low against the dollar for a second consecutive session on Friday to approach the psychological 57 mark, hurt by dollar demand from oil firms and gold importers, as well the broad risk-off sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Traders did not spot \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far, but most expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to step up dollar sales if the rupee ventures close to the 57 mark.\par \par \uc2 The RBI was suspected to have sold dollars on Thursday, though the actions were described by traders as "mild."\par \par \uc2 "The USD is strong across the board. I think at the higher levels exporters are unwinding their positions partially," said Uday Bhatt, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer with state-run \uc2 UCO Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee was at 56.96/97 per dollar, after hitting a record low of 56.97, and down nearly 1.2 percent from its Thursday's close of 56.30/31.\par \par \uc2 The rupee had hit its last record low at 56.55 just on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The past couple of sessions have resembled developments in May, when the rupee tumbled to a string of record lows, as renewed global risk aversion exposed India's fiscal and economic challenges.\par \par \uc2 Slowing policy reforms by a government facing faltering economic growth as well as current account and budget deficits has added to the rupee declines.\par \par \uc2 India's main share index was down 0.5 percent. The U.S. dollar was hovering near a one-week high against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after Moody's Investors Service downgraded 15 of the world's biggest banks.\par \par \uc2 "The broad risk aversion is hurting. There were bids from oil firms as oil prices are also lower while goldies also bought," said Vikas Babu Chittiprolu, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer with \uc2 Andhra Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Oil companies are the largest buyers of dollars in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as India imports nearly two-thirds of its oil needs. Brent crude was hovering around $90 a barrel, up slightly from an 18-month low hit on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The one-month offshore non-deliverable forward contracts were quoted at 57.40 while the three-month was at 58.16, suggesting a bearish near-term outlook for the rupee.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Swati Bhat; Editing by Rafael Nam)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-INDIA-RUPEE/ (UPDATE) | ABN | M | RBN | SNI | SNS | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120625e86m00076\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
