\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Hits Record Low; Bonds Gain As Crude Softens\b0\par\par\uc2 By Khushita Vasant   \par \uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2012\par 14:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                     At 1200 GMT \par                          Latest      Change \par    USD/INR                56.30       +0.15 \par    8.79% 2021 Bond        8.34%       -5BP \par    8.15% 2022 Bond        8.05%       -6BP \par    Call Rate              8.21%       -8BP \par    Forward Dollar Premium/Discount* \par    May                    2.9650      -0.045 \par    *Forward Dollar Premium/Discount are midpoints of bid-offer spreads \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MUMBAI--The Indian rupee slumped to its lowest level ever against the U.S. dollar Thursday, thanks to broad region-wide strength in the greenback after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  decided against monetary stimulus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at INR56.30 late Thursday in Asia, up from INR56.15 late Wednesday. It pared some gains after rising to an all-time high of INR56.55.\par \par \uc2 The rupee has depreciated more than 20% against the dollar over the past 12 months.\par \par \uc2 The rupee slid despite suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders said the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out soft \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or selling smaller dollar amounts, through the day.\par \par \uc2 But, the pair reversed direction only after the euro and local stock markets edged higher. The turnaround came through after Spain sold more than the planned 2.220 billion euros of government bonds at an auction.\par \par \uc2 Strategists say the rupee will be a troubled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for some more time.\par \par \uc2 Westpac\uc2  strategist Jonathan Cavenagh said in a note that the rupee hasn't been able to find much support despite improved risk appetite in recent days, even though the broader backdrop for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been reasonably supportive.\par \par \uc2 India's poor domestic situation--fiscal and current-account deficits, and sluggishness on reforms--coupled with poor news from ratings firms continue to outweigh any broader improvement in risk appetite, he said.\par \par \uc2 The RBI's decision to keep interest rates unchanged hasn't helped either.\par \par \uc2 "We still feel dollar-rupee will have a meaningful correction lower at some stage but it's likely we need to see more action on the domestic policy front before this transpires," Mr. Cavenagh said.\par \par \uc2 In the sovereign-debt market, bonds finished higher as global oil prices declined significantly.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures were lower Thursday, with Brent hitting its weakest price point in 18 months, after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  didn't announce aggressive monetary easing steps.\par \par \uc2 The new benchmark 8.15% 2022 bond finished at INR100.64, compared with INR100.25 at Wednesday's close. The 8.79% 2021 note was at INR102.85 compared with INR102.56.\par \par \uc2 Lower oil prices are positive for an import-dependent country like India. Further, it translates into softer inflation and lends more room for interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 The next trading cue comes from an INR120 billion buyback of bonds by the RBI Friday. The government will also sell INR150 billion worth of bonds.\par \par \uc2 Write to Khushita Vasant at khushita.vasant@dowjones.com [ 21-06-12 1244GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120621e86l000td\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
