\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  Economist Says  \highlight22\uc2 India Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Should Relook  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Strategy\b0\par\par\uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2012\par 13:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Rupee's 15% depreciation since March equals to monetary easing, says economist\par \par \uc2 -- More interest rate cuts will hurt rupee\par \par \uc2 -- Govt should raise foreign investment cap in local bonds, cut withholding tax\par \par \uc2 By Khushita Vasant\par \par \uc2 MUMBAI--India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to have another look at the way in which it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets because its recent dollar sales haven't been able to stop the rupee's slide, a senior economist said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a legitimate instrument, but the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't do it the right way," \uc2 Jahangir Aziz\uc2 , \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  senior Asia economist, told Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 "The RBI could have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a more strategic manner when the market was willing to accept it as a signal that the RBI is coming out and defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected of having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in recent weeks, but the rupee slumped to a series of record lows, with its worst being 56.51 against the U.S. dollar on May 31.\par \par \uc2 Other corrective policy steps didn't help much either.\par \par \uc2 The frequent dollar sales have been eating into India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which fell for a fourth consecutive week to $288.26 billion as at May 25. This is a sharp decline from $306.775 billion in early December.\par \par \uc2 While the reserves are at a relatively comfortable level for now, it would be a cause for concern if the RBI keeps up the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pace over the next few months, said Mr. Aziz, a former top adviser to \uc2 India's finance ministry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He believes the RBI shouldn't cut interest rates at this point. The rupee's depreciation of 15% since March and subsequent weakening against trading partner countries has made exports competitive. "That, in itself, is effectively equivalent to about 100-150 basis points of monetary easing."\par \par \uc2 Further rate cuts at a time when the rupee has been declining, when capital inflows are fairly sluggish and there is uncertainty overseas ahead of polls in Greece will only hurt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more.\par \par \uc2 The clamour for rate cuts has grown shriller in the past week after India reported a 5.3% gross domestic product growth for the January-March quarter -- the slowest pace in nearly 10 years.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Aziz said also that the government should raise the ceiling on foreign investments in local bonds to $50 billion from $15 billion. It could slash the withholding tax on bonds for investors to encourage participation from overseas investors.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Aziz thinks the greenback's rally is overshot, and it may correct 5%-6% in the near-term.\par \par \uc2 Write to Khushita Vasant at khushita.vasant@dowjones.com [ 06-06-12 1133GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120606e866000qm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
