\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET EYE FOCUS-Indian CD rates to rise on liquidity squeeze\b0\par\par\uc2 457 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Mai 2012\par 07:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  MUMBAI, May 29 (Reuters) - Short-term borrowing rates in India are expected to rise further in the next few weeks as companies pay advance taxes, exacerbating a severe cash shortage in the financial system.\par \par \uc2  Rates on 3-month certificates of deposit were at 9.70 percent on May 28, h igher than any of the first quarters in at least the previous three years, while borrowing rates for three-month commercial paper surged to 10.23 percent.  \par \par \uc2  Bankers expect 3-month CDs to go as high as 10.25 percent in June and the one-year CD as much as 10.30 percent.\par \par \uc2  Unlike years past, cash conditions have not improved significantly in April and May, as government spending that usually kicks in at the start of a new financial year has failed to have the usual impact given the structural deficit in liquidity in India's inter-bank system, analysts said.\par \par \uc2  Traders are now bracing for worse liquidity conditions as companies are expected to pay as much as 300 billion rupees ($5.41 billion)in advance taxes starting in mid-June.\par \par \uc2  That's especially the case given the \uc2 Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, though it has offset some of the impact on rupee liquidity by purchasing bonds via open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "Inter-bank liquidity is seen tightening more in June because of further \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , keeping an upward pressure on short-term CD rates," said N.S. Venkatesh, treasurer at \uc2 IDBI Bank\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The severe cash shortage in the banking system is clearly shown by banks' repo borrowings, which has averaged about 1 trillion rupees a day in the current quarter, well above the 650 billion rupees levels the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considers normal.\par \par \uc2  The RBI's bond purchases via OMOs as well as in secondary markets has also not been big enough to offset this liquidity deficit.\par \par \uc2  The RBI has bought 320.87 billion rupees via OMOs over the previous three weeks, and traders are waiting to see whether it will announce a fourth consecutive action this week.\par \par \uc2  Apart from fresh borrowing, banks are already refinancing existing CDs that mature towards the end of the quarter. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimate that up to 1.4 trillion rupees of CDs will be rolled over by the end of June, mostly at the short-end, all of which is expected to further worsen liquidity.\par \par \uc2  "Redemption and rollover pressure of CDs up to June, especially in three-month segment, will add to the pressure on short term rates," IDBIs Venkatesh said. ($1 = 55.4050 Indian rupees) \par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Archana Narayanan; Editing by Rafael Nam)\par \par \uc2 MARKETEYE-FOCUS/CD | ABN | D | M | SNI | SNS   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120529e85t0007r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
