\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/India Real Time: Rupee's Likely Defender: Bond, India Bond\b0\par\par\uc2 773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2012\par 11:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's India Real Time Report blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/indiarealtime"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/indiarealtime}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Shefali Anand\par \par \uc2 As the Indian rupee hovers near its lowest levels against the U.S. dollar, investors and analysts have been calling for a special bond sale to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was up from its nadir, trading at 55.36 rupees to the dollar. Last week, it had hit an all-time low of 56.37 rupees.\par \par \uc2 The rupee's recent strength was partly because of some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 But there are only so many rupees that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can buy.\par \par \uc2 To get fresh money into India, some economists suggest issuing a special type of bond targeted toward Indians who live abroad. Such bonds have been issued only three times in India's history, and have been a huge success in the past.\par \par \uc2 In 1991, they were called "India Development Bonds" and they raised $1.6 billion. In 1998, so-called "Resurgent India Bonds" raised $4.2 billion, and in 2000, India Millennium Deposits raised $5.5.billion. The proceeds from these sales were primarily meant to boost India's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and ultimately help India's balance of payments.\par \par \uc2 These were five-year bonds, issued in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the dollar or the British pound, and repaid in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So, the investor didn't have to worry about exchange rate risk. But it did ensure that the government got a lot of foreign money which it could hold for five years which added to India's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. These bonds have historically been issued by India's largest bank, \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2 , which is majority-owned by the government and thus carried an implicit government guarantee.\par \par \uc2 Economists say such bonds could come in handy now, at a time when foreign investors are skeptical of putting fresh money into India. They would be attractive if the bonds pay a better interest rate than whatever the non-resident Indians can get in their own countries.\par \par \uc2 These NRI bonds "can provide some kind of chunky inflows," says Siddhartha Sanyal, India economist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . "Eventually, our sense is that (RBI) would go for something like this."\par \par \uc2 But the size of such a bond issue would need to be much larger than the ones issued in the past, and its timing would have to be thought through.\par \par \uc2 "Will this be seen as a sign of a very, very difficult time for India?" says Sonal Varma, economist at Nomura in India. If so, the costs of issuing the bonds -- the interest rate the government pays on them -- could go up.\par \par \uc2 Another option to attract foreign capital quickly would be something that India has toyed with for a long time -- a sovereign bond issue. These would be bonds issued by the Indian government in dollars or another foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , available to foreign investors and traded in foreign markets.\par \par \uc2 At the moment, only foreign investors who are registered in India can buy Indian government bonds and these are rupee-denominated and can be traded only in India. Also, there are restrictions on how much Indian government debt foreigners can buy.\par \par \uc2 "RBI is always worried that too much of external debt is not a good thing," says Samiran Chakraborty, head of research for South Asia at Standard Chartered Bank.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Chakraborty notes that a decision to issue sovereign bonds would ultimately have to come from the government. Also, this may not be the best time to issue such bonds because India's weakening economic growth could force it to pay a higher interest rate to attract global investors. India's credit rating is at risk of losing its standing as high quality, which makes it less attractive to foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 RBI Governor Duvvuri Subbarao said last week that a bond sale is not on the cards right now.\par \par \uc2 Even if these bond sales happen, economists say they would only be a temporary help. The only sure way to boost the rupee is to address the major concerns for its decline, which include a widening current account deficit and lack of large foreign capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 "For a more sustained reversal in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you need Delhi to act as well," says Mr. Chakraborty.\par \par \uc2 You can follow Shefali and India Real Time on Twitter @shefalianand and @indiarealtime.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 28-05-12 0934GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120528e85s000i5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
